# `Option<T>` and absence

HQL has no null. A value that may not be there is `Option<T>`, an ordinary sum
type, and the reader gets something it must handle rather than something that
might surprise it. `Option` is not a trait, a bound or a special case in the
checker — see [[type-system]].

## A doc reference is optional by type

`[[name]]` is `Option<Card>` **by type**, not only when it fails. The vault is
not consulted to decide what the expression's type is, and a reference that does
resolve has the same type as one that does not.

```hql
alice   = @[[alice]]     // Option<Card>
nowhere = @[[nowhere]]   // Option<Card>, and absent
```

Evaluating an unresolved reference yields absence and queues a warning; the exit
status does not change. A forward link to a document nobody has written yet is
ordinary, which is why it is a warning rather than an error — see
[[error-handling]] and [[link-op]].

## Absence propagates

Absence lifts through a field access and through a step:

```hql
@[[nowhere]].title             // Option<String>
@[[nowhere]] | downlinks       // a step applied to nothing, not a failure
```

A step applied to absence is a step applied to nothing rather than a failure.
A step over a collection takes absence as the empty collection, so
`@[[nowhere]] | downlinks` is an empty `List<Edge>`. A step over any other
value is lifted over the option: `none | abs` is an absent `Option<Int>`,
`some(-2) | abs` the present `Option<Int>` holding `2`, and a step whose
result is already an `Option` gives that `Option`.

**This lifting is a stopgap.** Absence is an ordinary sum type consumed
explicitly — by an exhaustive [[match-op|match]], by `given … otherwise`, by
`somes`, or by the operations below — with no null check anywhere. `match`,
`given` and `somes` are implemented; the lifting stays beside them until the
corpus no longer needs it. `some(x)` builds a present option and `none` the
absent one.

## Optional fields

Reading an optional field is exactly an `Option`. `metadata? : Data` marks the
*field* optional and `born: Date?` the *type*; they stay distinct claims about
the schema, and both read as `Option<T>`. Reading a key of a
[[data-type|Data]] tree is optional by type for the same reason, and equality
looks through the `Option`, so `card.metadata.genre == "novel"` is `false` for a
card with no genre — see [[fields]].

Absence is not the answer to a wrong shape. A value that is present but is not
the type it was read as fails as a type error in the
[[error-handling|diagnostic channel]].

## Resolving absence

`Option<T>` comes with the established option vocabulary, declared beside it in
[`lib/std/core/mod.hql`](../../../../lib/std/core/mod.hql): `is_some`, `is_none`, `unwrap_or`,
`unwrap_or_else`, `alternative`, `map`, `and_then` and `filter`. There is no `unwrap`;
every way out of an `Option` says what absence means:

```hql
cards | map(c => c.metadata.genre.unwrap_or("unshelved"))
```

These are designed, not yet implemented — see
[optional fields and optional values](../../../models/behavior/options-in-types-and-fields.md).

## The present values of a collection

`somes` keeps the present values of a collection of options and drops the
absent ones:

```hql
somes<F<_> : Iterable<_>, T>(xs: F<Option<T>>) : F<T>
```

```hql
[some(1), none, some(3)] | somes     // [1, 3] : List<Int>
```

It returns the input's constructor applied to `T`, as `map` does, so a
`List<Option<T>>` gives a `List<T>` and a `Set<Option<T>>` a `Set<T>`. It is
the collection counterpart of `unwrap_or`: absence is dropped rather than
replaced. `somes` is implemented.

`Option` is not the only sum type. A computation that may have *failed* is
`Result<T, E>`, built the same way with the same kind of operations, so
absence never stands in for failure.

